  "passage": "A smart medicine box reminded patients when to take pills. It sent alerts to family members if doses were missed.\nFor one older patient, it was helpful. For another, it felt controlling. She said she wanted support, not surveillance.\nThe clinic changed how it introduced the device. Patients could choose who received alerts and how often.\nThe medicine box raised a question beyond health: when does care become monitoring?\nTechnology for safety should respect dignity. A reminder can help someone stay independent, but only if the person remains part of the decision.\nGood care asks for consent, not just compliance.",
